{{Economy of Balisca}}
'''''Ferrocarriles Baliscano''''' ([[w:trade name|doing business as]] '''Baliscan Railways Coop.''') is a [[W:Cooperative federation|cooperative federation]] in [[Balisca]], established in 1928 to coordinate the operation and maintenance of the [[Rail transport in Balisca|confederal railway network]], including [[w:Train#Passenger trains|passenger]] and [[w:rail freight transport|freight]] services, and {{W|infrastructure}}. Its cooperatives are also involved in the [[W:Construction industry|construction industry]], and are considered to be major [[W:Real estate development|property developers]], particularly with [[W:Transit-oriented development|transit-oriented development]] near stations. In accordance with the [[Low Impact Initiative]], rail cooperatives have played a central role in shaping [[W:Right to the city|equitable spatial development]] since the 1980s: station-adjacent housing blocks, local markets, and cooperative offices are built and maintained by [[Construction industry in Balisca|construction syndicates]]''.'' These developments prioritize [[W:Accessibility|accessibility]], minimization of ecological impacts, and [[W:Social utility|social utility]] over commercial value, often incorporating [[W:Public space|public spaces]], child care facilities and libraries.

From the development of the first railway in the 1850s to the outbreak of the [[Baliscan Revolution]], railways in Balisca consisted largely of [[W:Private railway|private operators]] with limited confederal support through the [[Confederal Railway Commission]]. Following the Revolution these private companies were [[W:Socialization (economics)|seized by their workers]], from which they rapidly transitioned to [[W:Social ownership|social ownership]] and becoming [[W:Worker cooperative|worker's cooperatives]]. Since federalization of the cooperatives in 1928, the [[Confederal government of Balisca|confederal government]] has invested heavily in the expansion of the rail networks, enabling rapid [[W:Dieselisation|dieselisation]] and later [[W:Railway electrification|electrification]]. High-speed rail service in Balisca was first introduced on 21 September 1969, with the completion of the ''[[Porciúncula–Jatiel high-speed rail line|Direto]]'' line, which revolutionized the rail travel and found immediate success.

== History ==

== Structure ==
The Baliscan Railway Group consists of five service-oriented operators (four passenger operators and one nationwide freight operator) and several non-service related cooperatives. Rather than monopolizing infrastructure and operations, Ferrocarriles Baliscano employs a federated [[W:Open-access operator|open-access model]]. Rail infrastructure—tracks, depots, stations—is considered the [[W:Public good|public commons]], maintained cooperatively by a confederal body. Passenger-service oriented operators maintain regional autonomy and collectively pool resources together to coordinate policies, infrastructure, and investments. Each of the regional railway operators are [[W:Cooperatives|cooperatives]] run by a combination of employees, passengers, and communal representatives and following a [[W:Social dividend|"community" or "social" dividend model]], in which profits accrued in each division are then redirected towards service improvements, employee benefits, sustainability projects, and local reinvestments within their communities.

These four passenger railway operators are divided into geographic regions of the [[Baliscan archipelago]], with most of their services confined to their respective operating region. However, there are overlaps across administrative divisions. For example, passenger services in [[Bayara]] and [[Estuaria]] both operate under ''Transoccidental'' train services, while the [[Transmatarrañya high speed rail line|service between]] [[Uxiu]] and [[San Marco]] uses ''Transoccidental'' rolling stock, but the track segment between is owned by ''[[Transmatarrañya]]''.

